{"answer":"No. At the permitted input n=40,\n\nn^2+n+41=40^2+40+41=1600+40+41=1681=41·41.\n\nBecause 1<41<1681, the value 1681 is composite. Thus n=40 is a counterexample, so the polynomial is not prime for every nonnegative integer n.","approaches":[{"description":"Refute the universal statement by finding an allowed input whose polynomial value is composite.","id":"factor-counterexample","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The allowed input n=40 gives 1681=41·41."},{"description":"Attempt to infer the universal claim from finitely many initial prime values.","id":"initial-pattern","outcome":"rejected","reason":"A finite pattern cannot prove a universal assertion, and n=40 supplies a counterexample."}],"change_log":["Revised the draft into a self-contained proof that explicitly establishes both domain membership of 40 and the nontrivial factorization of its value.","Recorded the logical counterexample step explicitly, addressing the audit objection that one composite value must occur at an input in the quantified domain.","Retained no unsupported citations, tool receipts, or proposed computational checks; tool_requests is empty.","No audit objections remain on the supplied arithmetic, domain, or counterexample reasoning."],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks, “Is n^2+n+41 prime for every nonnegative integer n?”","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"question-domain","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The statement to be tested asserts primality of n^2+n+41 for every nonnegative integer n.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by substitution and arithmetic in evaluate-forty.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"value-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"At n=40, n^2+n+41=1681.","step_ids":["evaluate-forty"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the factorization and inequalities in factor-value.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["value-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"composite-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The value of n^2+n+41 at n=40 is composite.","step_ids":["factor-value"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the counterexample argument in refute-universal.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["question-domain","composite-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"universal-claim-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"It is false that n^2+n+41 is prime for every nonnegative integer n.","step_ids":["refute-universal"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"evaluate-forty","justification":"Since 40 is nonnegative, it is in the stated domain. Direct calculation gives 40^2+40+41=1600+40+41=1681.","statement":"Evaluate the polynomial at the allowed input n=40."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["evaluate-forty"],"id":"factor-value","justification":"1681=41·41, and 1<41<1681. Hence 1681 has a positive divisor other than 1 and itself, so it is composite.","statement":"Show that the value at n=40 is composite."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["evaluate-forty","factor-value"],"id":"refute-universal","justification":"A universal assertion over a domain is false when an element of that domain fails the asserted property. Here 40 is nonnegative and its polynomial value is composite, hence not prime.","statement":"Use n=40 as a counterexample to the universal assertion."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
